  "Question": "Let \\(f\\) be the function defined by \\(f(x)=ax^2-\\sqrt2\\) for some positive \\(a.\\) If \\(f(f(\\sqrt2))=-\\sqrt2,\\) then \\(a=\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\frac{2-\\sqrt2}{2}\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\frac12\\)",
    "C": "\\(2-\\sqrt2\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\frac{2+\\sqrt2}{2}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "Evaluate the inner function first, keeping the resulting square unexpanded:\n\\[\nf(\\sqrt2)=a(\\sqrt2)^2-\\sqrt2=2a-\\sqrt2.\n\\]\nThe given condition therefore becomes\n\\[\na(2a-\\sqrt2)^2-\\sqrt2=-\\sqrt2,\n\\]\nso\n\\[\na(2a-\\sqrt2)^2=0.\n\\]\nBecause \\(a>0\\), division by \\(a\\) is valid. A real square equals zero only when its base is zero, giving\n\\[\n2a-\\sqrt2=0,\n\\qquad a=\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}.\n\\]\nThis value is positive and gives \\(f(\\sqrt2)=0\\), followed by \\(f(0)=-\\sqrt2\\), as required. Thus \\(a=\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{D}\\).",
